{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And he placed him in the house of the prison.</b> It was not a capital case, for there were no witnesses in the matter, but only to keep him in custody." "<b>Prisoners of the king.</b> Written <b>אסורי</b>, read <b>אסירי</b>. And it is found in the Aggadah that they brought Joseph before the king. Gabriel came in the likeness of a man and said: if it seems good to the king, let him order that their garments be examined — if the woman's garments are torn, it is known that Joseph seized her to force her, and if Joseph's garments are torn, it was she who seized him to force him. They investigated the matter and found that Joseph's garments were torn, and on that account they did not sentence him to death. Even so, he was not released immediately, so as not to shame Potiphar's wife — so that it would not be said: indeed the matter became known, that she forced herself upon Joseph. And the priests of Egypt judged this case, and therefore Joseph did not acquire their land during the years of famine."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:08:03.933345325Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}